== What This Guide Is

*Primal Quarry* is a structured, three-wave playthrough framework for *Monster Hunter Rise* with the *Sunbreak* expansion — game version *16.0.3.1*, PC (Steam). It pairs a full how-to-play guide with a curated modlist that is installed through *Fluffy Manager 5000*.

Each wave is a complete new-game setup: a fresh save, a fresh mood, and its own mod subset. The waves are the story of your time at the quarry:

+ *Wave 0 — The Rookie's Oath.* A complete beginner's guide to how Monster Hunter actually works. Mods in this wave are vanilla-plus only: UI and quality-of-life tools that make the game easier to read and operate, never easier to win.
+ *Wave 1 — The Expedition.* Deeper strategy for hunters who know the basics: advanced combat, skills and builds, and the systems Sunbreak adds. Mods here add content and new mechanics — with the hard rule that nothing may be a pure power spike.
+ *Wave 2 — Warden of the Quarry.* Endgame mastery: anomaly investigations, risen monsters, augment crafting, and long-term goals. The philosophy from Wave 1 carries over.

The wave guides are written so that everything you need for *your* wave is in that wave's chapters; earlier chapters teach the fundamentals, later chapters assume them.

=== What You Need

- *Monster Hunter Rise* plus the *Sunbreak* expansion, updated to the latest 16.x patch (this guide is written against 16.0.3.1).
- *Fluffy Manager 5000* — installed and configured per Part 1, Setup.
- The wave's mod archives — supplied by you, the owner of this list (see Part 2 for the entries).
- One save slot per wave. Rise gives you multiple slots; keep the waves from bleeding into each other.

=== How to Read Mod Entries

Every mod in this guide is presented as a card with four fields:

- *Dependencies* — other mods or frameworks the mod needs (install those first).
- *System / mechanic impact* — what the mod actually changes in the game.
- *Notes* — load order, configuration, or anything else worth knowing.
- Optionally *Version* — the specific mod release this entry refers to.

The list is curated entirely by the owner. Per project rules, this project never researches, validates, or adds mods — every card records exactly what the owner supplied.

#note[
  Wave-specific mods may change mechanics this guide describes. When a mod touches something explained here, its card is the authority — trust the card, and test in-game after installing.
]

=== Housekeeping Rules

- *Back up your saves* before changing mods. Mods modify the game folder, not your save files, but a broken install is far easier to fix when the saves are safe.
- *Re-check the game version* after every game update. A new patch can break mods that were fine the day before; if Steam updates the game, confirm it still reads 16.0.3.1 and re-test your wave's mods.
- *Never enable mods from two waves at once* unless a card explicitly says it is safe. The waves are designed as distinct setups.
